From 2298d8329256819c0367557cd29364bc5412223b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 11:41:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 66/71] inline-3d(#90): retract inline-3D demand on navigation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 3, Stage 0. Two defects left the display-mode policy latched at the
wrong answer across a navigation. Both are browser-side only; nothing in the
runtime or the extension wire protocol moves.

C-1 — navigating away from a 3D page left the panel in 3D (the reported bug).
A document put in the back/forward cache keeps its DisplayXRService pipe, so
its demand is never retracted, and its RenderFrameHost is kInBackForwardCache
rather than gone. ComputeWish() already discounted it — IsActive() is false —
but nothing on a same-tab navigation ever asked: the controller only woke on
demand events and browser create/close/activate/deactivate/active-tab-change.
Renderer-side retraction is not available (XRSystem::EnsureDisplayXRService
returns null once the execution context is destroyed), so the browser has to
notice by itself.

  Fix: a WebContentsObserver on the active tab of each window, overriding
  PrimaryPageChanged() to ScheduleApply(). That fires on cross-document
  commits, bfcache restores and prerender activations, and deliberately not on
  same-document navigations, which a live inline-3D session is expected to
  survive. Only active tabs are observed — a background tab cannot change
  ComputeWish()'s answer — and the set is refreshed from the existing
  RegisterActiveTabDidChange path, so a tab dragged between windows is picked
  up by the window it lands in.

C-2 — latent permanent-stuck-2D race, fixed here because C-1's fix makes
navigation a hot path. On a same-RFH cross-document navigation the new
document binds a fresh pipe with the SAME GlobalRenderFrameHostId, and the old
pipe's destructor retraction is unordered against the new pipe's first
SetInline3DDemand(true). If the retraction lands second it erases the NEW
document's claim, and since the renderer is edge-triggered
(inline_3d_demand_sent_) it never re-reports: a 3D page stuck in 2D for the
life of the document.

  Fix: a generation token. DisplayXRServiceImpl mints instance_id_ from a
  process-global counter in its constructor, Inline3DDemandCallback carries it,
  and demanding_frames_ becomes id -> generation. A demand is insert_or_assign
  (newest claimant wins); a retraction erases only when the stored generation
  matches the retracting pipe's. A stale retraction is dropped instead of
  clearing a live claim.

Hardware test plan (Leia SR panel, --enable-inline-3d):
  1. Load a 3D page, confirm the panel goes 3D; navigate away. Panel returns
     to 2D within the 300 ms debounce (<=300 ms after commit).
  2. Press Back so the 3D document is restored from the bfcache. Panel returns
     to 3D — proves the observer fires on restore, not just on commit, and
     that the bfcached claim was discounted rather than destroyed.
  3. Navigate 3D page -> 3D page, same site, x20. Panel stays 3D throughout,
     with no instance ending stuck in 2D. This is the C-2 proof: same-site
     keeps the RenderFrameHost, so every hop runs the retraction/report race.
  4. Regression: two windows, 3D page in each, alternate focus; and a 3D page
     in a background tab of the foreground window. Policy unchanged.

Compile-checked (clang-cl -fsyntax-only, milestone-150 tree): the two edited
TUs plus both DisplayXRServiceImpl::Create call sites —
components/displayxr/browser/displayxr_service_impl.cc,
chrome/browser/displayxr/displayxr_display_mode_controller.cc,
chrome/browser/chrome_browser_interface_binders.cc,
content/shell/browser/shell_content_browser_client.cc. All clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
